# DianaSh golden corpus. Recorded from the C# shell, byte for byte; EMUSEN_DIANASH_GOLDEN=record rewrites it - see EmuSen_DianaSh.md §13.4.
# Patterns: <ROOT> is the sandbox's real root, <TEMP> the corpus's real temporary folder, <INSTALL> the install's real directory;
# a 'match' block is a regular expression the whole output must match, written where a line prints a time or a measured number.
command pkg
shell rust
usage 1   pkg make <path>... [--self-contained] [--zip]
usage 2                                 a package for each game under each path (relative to the library), linked
usage 3                                 unless the game is to be copied in; --zip also writes the package as one zip
usage 4   pkg ls [<console>]            the packages, with mode and state (ok, moved, missing, changed)
usage 5   pkg check [<package>...]      the game and every file hashed against the manifest (relative to Packages); a package's zip too
usage 6   pkg migrate <package>...      the game's saves, states, records, settings, art and cheats copied in
usage 7   pkg patch add <package> <file>
usage 8                                 an IPS, UPS or BPS patch checked, copied in and applied at load
usage 9   pkg patch rm <package> <n>    patch n taken out of the package's list
usage 10   pkg zip <package>...          a self-contained package written as one zip beside it, saves and all
usage 11   pkg identity <package> original|patched
usage 12                                 which identity the scraper sends for a patched package
usage 13   pkg wait [pkg<n>]             follow a pkg job that went on in the background

case the usage, and what a command line refuses
target none
covers 1-13
$ pkg
out 376
Usage: pkg make <path>... [--self-contained] [--zip] | pkg ls [<console>] | pkg check [<package>...] | pkg migrate <package>... | pkg patch add <package> <file> | pkg patch rm <package> <n> | pkg zip <package>... | pkg identity <package> original|patched | pkg wait [pkg<n>]
  Paths are relative to the library, / for all of it; packages are relative to Packages. See man pkg.
status 1
action none
$ pkg frobnicate
out 376
Usage: pkg make <path>... [--self-contained] [--zip] | pkg ls [<console>] | pkg check [<package>...] | pkg migrate <package>... | pkg patch add <package> <file> | pkg patch rm <package> <n> | pkg zip <package>... | pkg identity <package> original|patched | pkg wait [pkg<n>]
